Bind-mount MOUNT_SPEC inside the running sandbox.

MOUNT_SPEC is HOST[:CTR_TARGET[:ro|rw]] in docker -v style:

  HOST                      Bind-mount HOST at the same path inside the
                            running container — the same convention the
                            create-time workspace mount uses, so in-container
                            paths match host paths. On Windows the drive
                            letter becomes a root directory (C:\Users\me\data
                            → /c/Users/me/data).
  HOST:CTR_TARGET           Bind-mount the host path at CTR_TARGET instead
                            (real bind mount via setns + open_tree +
                            move_mount, not a symlink).
  HOST:CTR_TARGET:ro|rw     Mount read-only (:ro) or read-write (:rw, the
                            default).

HOST may be absolute or relative; relative paths are resolved against the
current working directory before submission, and ./.. segments are cleaned.
HOST must exist on the host. CTR_TARGET must be absolute. Mount operations
are idempotent: re-running the same MOUNT_SPEC is a no-op success.

Mounts are restored after stop/start. If a saved mount cannot be restored,
startup fails; restore its host path or use sbx umount while stopped to remove
the saved mount.

Mount policy (governance) is enforced before the call reaches the daemon, mirroring
the create-time check, and uses the effective read-only intent so policies
that distinguish ro/rw match what the bind mount does.

Usage:
  sbx mount SANDBOX MOUNT_SPEC [flags]

Examples:
  # Bind-mount at the same path as on the host
  sbx mount my-sandbox /Users/me/extra-data

  # Bind-mount inside the container at /workspace/data (read-write)
  sbx mount my-sandbox /Users/me/extra-data:/workspace/data

  # Bind-mount read-only (writes inside the container return EROFS)
  sbx mount my-sandbox /Users/me/extra-data:/workspace/data:ro

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for mount

Global Flags:
      --cloud   Dispatch to Docker Cloud Sandboxes API instead of local sandboxd (supported by a growing set of verbs — run 'sbx --cloud --help' for the current list)
  -D, --debug   Enable debug logging
